## Schema, Prompting, and Validation

A diagram tool lives or dies by its **contract**. The LLM output should never be free-form prose that is “somehow interpreted” into a graph. Instead, the LLM should always emit a **strict diagram spec** that the server can validate. NVIDIA’s structured-generation documentation specifically recommends `guided_json` and says it is preferable to `response_format={"type":"json_object"}` because plain JSON-object mode still allows the model to emit any valid JSON, including empty objects. NVIDIA also recommends `xgrammar`-backed guided decoding for reliability and performance. citeturn25view0

A practical contract has three layers: **structural validity**, **semantic validity**, and **render validity**. Structural validity means the JSON matches the schema. Semantic validity means the graph makes sense: edge endpoints exist, sequence steps are ordered, groups are not cyclic, and diagram type rules are respected. Render validity means the layout and renderer have enough information to produce a deterministic artifact, especially widths, heights, ports, and label constraints. React Flow SSR makes this last layer explicit because server-side rendering requires dimensions and handle locations up front. citeturn10search1turn0search1

