### Prioritized milestones

| Milestone | Scope | Estimated effort | Principal risks |
|---|---|---:|---|
| **Contract and schema** | Finalize DiagramSpec JSON schema, Pydantic models, semantic rules | 10–14 hours | Over-designing the schema before real prompts are tested |
| **NIM integration** | Add NIM adapter, model discovery, structured generation, repair loop | 8–12 hours | Model/profile mismatch; structured decoding behavior varies by model/profile |
| **Validation pipeline** | Structural + semantic validation + ambiguity handling | 12–18 hours | False positives on valid diagrams; insufficient clarification path |
| **Layout service** | ELK.js first, Dagre fallback, port-aware routing | 14–22 hours | Edge routing and group layout complexity |
| **Renderer and exports** | Canonical SVG renderer, PNG rasterization, PPTX packaging | 16–24 hours | Font consistency and export fidelity across environments |
| **Frontend editor** | React Flow preview, manual edits, regenerate/export actions | 14–20 hours | Drift between preview rendering and export rendering |
| **Hardening and tests** | Golden specs, visual diffing, CI, observability, error budgets | 18–28 hours | Snapshot flakiness, browser/font differences, version churn |
| **Visio bridge** | draw.io XML or native VSDX generator | 16–30 hours | Format complexity and interoperability edge cases |

A realistic MVP that stops at **SVG/PNG/PPTX** is usually achievable in roughly **70–110 engineering hours**. Adding a credible VSDX path often pushes the total toward **90–140 hours**, depending on how editable the output must be.

### CI/CD and layout-stability strategy

The most important testing principle is that you should test **the contract, the layout, and the pixels separately**. For contract tests, pin sample prompts and expected JSON outputs. For layout tests, normalize layouted JSON and assert stable coordinates, route topology, and edge endpoint validity. For pixel tests, use Playwright snapshots only after you have pinned fonts, dimensions, and export dependencies. Playwright’s official docs support screenshot-based visual comparisons, multi-project browser matrices, and CI execution directly. citeturn24search0turn24search2turn24search11turn24search6

A practical test matrix is below.

| Test layer | Recommended tool | Stability tactic |
|---|---|---|
| Prompt-to-spec | Unit tests + fixed prompts | Use `temperature=0`; validate against schema; record clarifications path |
| LLM reproducibility | NIM deterministic mode where supported | NVIDIA documents `NIM_FORCE_DETERMINISTIC=1` for reproducible outputs on supported TensorRT-LLM buildable profiles, with some latency/throughput cost. citeturn27view0 |
| Model/runtime pinning | Deployment config | Use explicit NIM profile IDs for deterministic, version-safe profile selection. citeturn26search5 |
| Layout output | JSON golden files | Canonicalize node order, round coordinates, and diff the graph model before rendering |
| Render output | Playwright snapshots | Fix viewport, fonts, theme tokens, and browser version; compare SVG DOM and PNG snapshots. citeturn24search0turn24search2 |
| Export pipeline | Integration tests | Rasterize the same SVG in CI and verify dimensions and checksum tolerances |
| Client-side convenience export | Optional smoke test only | React Flow’s example pins `html-to-image` 1.11.11 because later versions were problematic; pin it if you keep a client export path. citeturn0search5 |

For React Flow SSR and export stability, always persist explicit **node width/height** and explicit **handle placement** into the post-layout JSON, because React Flow’s SSR docs make clear that the server cannot discover these at runtime. That requirement should be treated as a schema rule, not an implementation detail. citeturn0search1

For progressive delivery, the safest release pattern is **feature flags by export target**. Release SVG and PNG first, then PPTX, then Visio. Each export target should have its own golden tests because compatibility downgrades are normal: for example, PptxGenJS documents that SVG images are supported in newer desktop PowerPoint or Microsoft 365, so a production exporter should be able to fall back to PNG automatically when compatibility matters more than editability. citeturn22view0

The minimal end-to-end prototype, therefore, should prioritize this stack: **FastAPI + Pydantic backend, NVIDIA NIM with `guided_json`, ELK.js layout, React Flow preview/editor, direct SVG renderer, Sharp/Puppeteer for PNG, PptxGenJS for PPTX, and Playwright for visual regression**. That combination matches the documented strengths of the underlying systems while keeping the architecture narrow enough to stabilize in a first release. citeturn10search2turn10search1turn25view0turn0search2turn2search7turn11search2turn11search3turn22view0turn24search0
